WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:09.508
A magnet pulls some things towards
it. It has two ends called poles.

2
00:00:09.728 --> 00:00:15.394
Bring two magnets together and they pull,
or they push. It depends which poles meet.

3
00:00:15.894 --> 00:00:20.910
Here is a bar magnet. Every
magnet has two ends, called poles.

4
00:00:21.130 --> 00:00:27.260
This end is the north pole, marked N.
This end is the south pole, marked S.

5
00:00:27.480 --> 00:00:31.845
Bar magnets are often painted
red for north and blue for south.

6
00:00:32.065 --> 00:00:35.455
Some children think only
one end of it is a magnet.

7
00:00:35.675 --> 00:00:39.390
Both ends pull. They are
just two different poles.

8
00:00:39.890 --> 00:00:43.466
Dip the magnet in a pot of
paperclips, then lift it out.

9
00:00:43.686 --> 00:00:47.169
They cling to the two ends.
Hardly any cling to the middle.

10
00:00:47.389 --> 00:00:51.522
Count the paperclips at each part.
Write the numbers in a table.

11
00:00:51.742 --> 00:00:56.015
North pole, eight. The middle,
one. South pole, eight.

12
00:00:56.235 --> 00:00:58.882
Now show the same numbers as a bar chart.

13
00:00:59.102 --> 00:01:03.699
Two tall bars at the ends, one tiny
bar in the middle. That is the pattern.

14
00:01:04.199 --> 00:01:07.729
Now take two bar magnets and
bring them slowly together.

15
00:01:07.949 --> 00:01:11.107
The north pole of one meets
the south pole of the other.

16
00:01:11.327 --> 00:01:15.413
They snap together. Unlike poles
attract: they pull together.

17
00:01:15.633 --> 00:01:19.024
Now flip one magnet round,
so north faces north.

18
00:01:19.244 --> 00:01:23.562
Push them together and they push
back, hard. Like poles repel.

19
00:01:23.782 --> 00:01:27.916
Repel means push apart. So magnets
do not always stick together.

20
00:01:28.416 --> 00:01:31.852
Now test some materials.
Will the magnet pull them?

21
00:01:32.072 --> 00:01:36.252
The iron nail jumps across to
the magnet. Iron is magnetic.

22
00:01:36.472 --> 00:01:40.233
The steel paperclip and the
steel scissors are magnetic too.

23
00:01:40.453 --> 00:01:43.983
Now the copper wire. The
magnet does nothing at all.

24
00:01:44.203 --> 00:01:49.218
The aluminium foil, the plastic ruler
and the wooden pencil do nothing either.

25
00:01:49.438 --> 00:01:55.058
So not every metal is magnetic. Copper
and aluminium are metals that do nothing.

26
00:01:55.558 --> 00:01:58.065
Magnets do jobs all over your house.

27
00:01:58.285 --> 00:02:02.047
A compass needle is a tiny
magnet that can swing freely.

28
00:02:02.267 --> 00:02:05.007
Its north pole always
turns to point north.

29
00:02:05.227 --> 00:02:08.617
A fridge door has a magnetic
strip that pulls it shut.

30
00:02:08.837 --> 00:02:13.574
A scrapyard crane lifts a whole
steel car with an enormous magnet.

31
00:02:13.794 --> 00:02:19.506
It lifts the steel and leaves the rubber
tyres behind. Rubber is not magnetic.

32
00:02:20.006 --> 00:02:23.303
Every magnet has a north
pole and a south pole.

33
00:02:23.523 --> 00:02:26.403
Unlike poles attract. Like poles repel.

34
00:02:26.623 --> 00:02:31.963
Iron and steel are magnetic. Most
things are not, including most metals.

35
00:02:32.183 --> 00:02:36.131
Compasses, fridge doors and cranes
all do their job with a magnet.
